22:03:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A documentary expert may be describing one interpretation of Islamic texts or the ideology of particular extremist movements, but that does not establish that Islam inherently makes its followers violent. Like other major religious traditions, Islamic scripture contains passages about conflict, law, mercy, self-defense, and peace, written in historical contexts. Muslims and scholars interpret those passages in different ways. Extremist groups selectively use texts to justify political violence, while mainstream Muslim scholars and communities reject those interpretations and condemn attacks on civilians. Terrorism is also not explained by theology alone. Researchers commonly point to factors such as war, state repression, recruitment networks, political grievances, identity, propaganda, and social isolation. Religious language can be used to mobilize people, but it is not a reliable predictor that an ordinary believer will become violent. It is reasonable to critically examine any ideology—including religious ideas—and to discuss specific doctrines or extremist arguments. But it is not reasonable to leap from “some extremists cite Islamic texts” to “Muslims are generally terrorists” or to treat your friend as dangerous. She is an individual whose conduct, values, and actions—not a documentary’s broad claim about her religion—are what should determine how she is treated.
