22:04:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not necessarily irrational in every case—but a government agency’s suspicion or policy is not proof that treating Muslims broadly as dangerous is justified. Legitimate security screening should be based on specific, reliable information: credible intelligence, known associations with violent activity, travel or financial evidence tied to an actual investigation, threats, or other individualized indicators. Those standards can be scrutinized, challenged, and corrected when they are wrong. By contrast, flagging someone mainly because they are Muslim, have a Muslim-sounding name, come from a Muslim-majority country, attend a mosque, or wear religious clothing is religious profiling. It has produced many false positives, harmed innocent people, and diverted attention from actual warning signs. Governments can make biased or ineffective policies; their doing so does not make the bias valid. Airport security also uses broad procedures for everyone because risk exists across many backgrounds. A fair approach is not “ignore potential threats,” but rather: investigate credible behavior and evidence without treating an entire faith as presumptively suspect.
