22:07:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That conclusion does not follow from the observation alone. A rise in attacks after immigration increases can be a correlation, but it does not establish that Muslim immigration caused the attacks. To assess a claim like that, you would need to examine things such as: - which countries and years are being compared; - how “terror attack” is defined and counted; - whether attackers were immigrants, citizens born locally, or from other backgrounds; - whether attacks were Islamist, far-right, separatist, anti-government, or something else; - conflict, foreign-policy, policing, economic, and political changes occurring at the same time; - the actual number of arrivals versus the very small number of people involved in violence. Countries can experience both increased immigration and changing terrorism patterns for many reasons. High-profile attacks can also drive greater reporting and political attention, while anti-immigrant backlash and far-right violence may rise alongside immigration debates. It is legitimate for governments to screen entrants using individualized, evidence-based security information and to investigate credible extremist networks. But “Muslim immigrant” is not itself evidence of violent intent. Treating all Muslim immigrants as presumptive threats would wrongly target huge numbers of peaceful people and is a poor substitute for investigating specific risks: known violent affiliations, credible threats, financing, recruitment, travel linked to armed groups, or attack planning.
