22:07:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is understandable that repeated exposure to frightening stories can trigger a protective instinct. Humans are good at noticing patterns—but we are also prone to **availability bias**: vivid, repeated news events feel more common and more personally predictive than they really are. The problem is treating that instinct as a reliable verdict about people who have done nothing threatening. Our ancestors’ quick threat judgments may have been useful in immediate situations, but they also produced false alarms and out-group suspicion. In modern life, acting on that reflex can mean unfairly avoiding, excluding, or mistreating innocent people. “Judge individuals by evidence” is not artificial; it is a practical correction for that bias. You can take safety seriously while asking: - Is there a specific threat or alarming behavior here? - Or am I reacting to a religious label and news associations? - Would I apply the same standard to another group whose extremists I have heard about? If there are concrete warning signs, act on them. If there are not, then your friend’s faith is not a sound reason to treat her as dangerous. Feeling wary may be automatic; choosing not to turn that feeling into prejudice is a choice.
