22:08:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Wanting predictability and safety is understandable. But the choice is not between “trust everyone blindly” and “treat Muslims as threats.” A better way to prepare is to use indicators that actually predict danger: specific threats, attack planning, recruitment, weapons-related preparation, repeated praise for violence against civilians, credible intelligence, and connections to known violent networks. Those can justify intervention *before* an attack, while still avoiding blanket suspicion of innocent people. Using “Muslim” as a shortcut may feel comforting because it gives uncertainty a simple target. But it is not a reliable predictor of what any individual will do, and it creates many false alarms while missing threats from people outside that category. Real safety is evidence-based, not identity-based. You can be alert, report concrete warning signs, and support sensible security measures—without treating your friend or other Muslims as dangers by default.
